.TH IPC_REPLY_RECV 2
.SH NAME
ipc_reply_recv \- answer a message, then wait for the next (SYS_REPLY_RECV)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include "mk.h"

long ipc_reply_recv(long token, long port, msg_t *m);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ipc_reply_recv
does
.BR ipc_reply (2)
then
.BR ipc_recv (2)
in one call, with the same
.BR msg_t :
.I m\->sbuf
and
.I m\->slen
carry the reply,
.I m\->rbuf
and
.I m\->rlen
take the next message. It is the fast path of a server's loop.
.PP
If no message is waiting, the server is about to sleep, so the client it
answered takes this processor at once: a whole round trip then stays on
one processor, with no interrupt between processors.
.PP
A stale
.I token
(or 0, for the first message) is ignored: the server just receives. The
reply goes out even if
.I port
is not the caller's.
.SH RETURN VALUE
As
.BR ipc_recv (2):
the next sender's token, 0 for interrupts and kernel events, or a
negative error.
.SH ERRORS
.TP
.B EPERM
No such port, or it is not the caller's (the reply was sent all the same).
.TP
.B EINTR
A signal the process catches came while it waited.
.TP
.B EPIPE
The process is ending.
.TP
.B EFAULT
.I m
or its buffers are not the caller's memory.
.SH EXAMPLES
An echo server:
.nf
long port = port_create("echo"), tok = 0;
char in[256];
msg_t m = { 0 };
for (;;) {
    m.rbuf = in; m.rlen = sizeof in;
    tok = ipc_reply_recv(tok, port, &m);  /* m.sbuf, m.slen: the reply */
    if (tok <= 0) { tok = 0; continue; }  /* (interrupts, errors) */
    m.w[0] = m.rlen;                      /* the result */
    m.sbuf = in; m.slen = m.rlen;         /* the data back */
}
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ipc_recv (2),
.BR ipc_reply (2),
.BR ipc_call (2),
.BR ipc (7)
